      integerIntro:'Without mod p, f(x) mod x reveals s mod x.', integerExample:'Integer shares expose remainders:',
      invalidInput:'Enter an integer from 0 to p−1.', kOne:'k=1: every share equals the secret. This is just copying, not splitting.',
      fieldRule:'Addition in GF(2⁸) is XOR. For multiplication, reduce modulo m(z)=z⁸+z⁴+z³+z+1. The coefficients form binary 1 0001 1011, written 0x11B in hexadecimal.',
      byteFieldReason:'Because m(z) is irreducible, every nonzero byte has an inverse for interpolation. This tool uses the same m(z) as FIPS 197 for AES. It does not set the degree of the share polynomial Fⱼ(X). See “The GF(2⁸) reduction polynomial” in Help for the reason.',
      byteCalculationTitle:'How each share byte is calculated', byteCalculationIntro:'The current parameters are (k,n)=({k},{n}). For each byte position j, form a polynomial Fⱼ(X) of degree at most k−1 and evaluate X=1 through {n} to calculate the shares. For (3,5), Fⱼ(X)=sⱼ⊕a₁,ⱼX⊕a₂,ⱼX².', byteRandomHow:'The dealer uses original byte sⱼ as the constant term and draws the other k−1 coefficients independently for each byte position. Each coefficient is a uniformly chosen random byte from 00 through FF, drawn with the browser’s crypto.getRandomValues. Zero is possible.', byteRandomWhy:'Random coefficients ensure that k−1 or fewer shares do not narrow down the original byte. Reloading the page or changing the input, k, or n draws fresh coefficients and creates new shares. Switching the inspected byte position does not redraw them. Coefficients are shown for learning; each participant receives only their own share.', byteColumnLabel:'Byte position to inspect', byteCoefficientSource:'“{position}: {secret}” means that original byte s{sub}={secret} is at zero-based position j={position}. This run drew {coefficients} for that position. These are hexadecimal bytes, not values calculated from s{sub}. In a₁,{sub}, the first index gives the power of X and the second gives byte position {position}; it does not mean “for share #1”. Shares #1 through #{n} use the same coefficients, substituting their identifiers X=01 through {last}.', byteFirstExample:'For share #1, substitute X=01: {expression}. This is the calculation in the first table row.', byteCalcShare:'Share', byteCalcSecret:'Original byte', byteCalcResult:'XOR result',
      byteInput:'Short phrase (up to 32 UTF-8 bytes)', byteDistribute:'Distribution: create byte shares', byteRestore:'Reconstruction: recover the text from k shares',
      byteSecret:'Original bytes', byteShare:'Share', byteWeight:'λᵢ(0)', restoredHex:'Recovered hex', restoredText:'Recovered text',
      byteNoSelection:'none', byteSelectionStatus:'Selected shares: {shares} ({count}/{k})', byteSelectFirst:'Select one share to reveal its equation for the first byte (position 0).',
      bytePendingWhy:'The {k} unknowns for the first byte are {unknowns}. The selected {count} shares provide only {count} equations. Every value from 00 through FF is still possible for s₀.',
      byteWeightsIntro:'Now k shares are available. Interpolate the first byte (position 0) at x=0. Nᵢ is the product of the other X values; Dᵢ is the product of XORs between this X and each other X. Subtraction is XOR in GF(2⁸), so λᵢ(0)=Nᵢ×Dᵢ⁻¹.',
      byteRecoveryValue:'First share byte', byteRecoveryProduct:'λᵢ(0)×share byte', byteRecoveryExample:'Recover the first byte: s₀ = {terms} = {result}. Apply the same λᵢ(0) values to every remaining byte position.',
